window.docContents[247].resolve({"/Tier-1___-statements/Statement-files-_LPAR_T_RPAR_/Computational.Specifications.Pqxdh.Security.Auth.Game/#file-Computational___Specifications___Pqxdh___Security___Auth___Game":{"contents":"Paper › Computational/Specifications/Pqxdh/Security/Auth › Game.leanTier 1 · statement file\n\nTier 1 · statement file used by the computational proof 1 headline theorem depend on it 985 lines trust banner B1, trusted: the statement source ↗Read in full. A statement file: its definitions say what the headline theorems claim (games, adversaries, bounds). Proofs are cut after := and shown as ⋯; Lean checks them.\n\nDeclarations on this page (61)structure AeadIndCpaAdvdef AeadIndCpaAdv.Boundeddef aeadIndCpaExpdef AeadIndCpainductive PrfHmacQuerydef prfHmacSpecstructure PrfHmacAdvdef PrfHmacAdv.Boundeddef prfHmacExpdef PrfHmacstructure PrfCkAdvdef PrfCkAdv.Boundeddef prfCkExpdef PrfChainKeydef effectiveHkdfSaltBytesinductive PrfMsgKeysQuerydef prfMsgKeysSpecstructure PrfMsgKeysAdvdef PrfMsgKeysAdv.Boundeddef prfMsgKeysExpdef PrfMsgKeysdef evalRootKdfOrRostructure SentOPKEventstructure SentNoOPKEventstructure RecvdOPKEventstructure RecvdNoOPKEventdef matchSentOPKdef matchSentNoOPKstructure AuthInitiatorEntrydef authInitCorruptionClauseinductive AuthQueryinductive AuthResponsedef authSpecstructure AuthAdversarystructure AuthStatedef AuthState.emptydef runAuthSenddef consumeMatchingSentOPKdef consumeMatchingSentNoOPKdef pqxdhAuthImpldef pqxdhAuthExpCoredef pqxdhAuthExpdef AuthAdvantagedef isAuthRoQueryinstance L836def isAuthRoRootQueryinstance L845def isAuthSendQueryinstance L855def isAuthRecvQueryinstance L865def isAuthOnlineQueryinstance L878instance L883def rootKdfRoTermdef pqxdhAuthBounddef pqxdhAuthExpCoreWiththeorem pqxdhAuthExpCore_eq_withdef pqxdhAuthImpl1Keydef pqxdhAuth1KeyExpdef Auth1KeyAdvantage\n\n\n\nThe one-key first-message Auth game (Lemma 1, second conjunct)Trusted: the statement. Every definition in this file is part of the one-key first-message authentication and privacy statement SignalPqxdh.Security.PqxdhAuth1KeySecureδ (Security/Auth/Headline.lean), the second conjunct of PqxdhSecureδ in Lemma 1 (SignalPqxdh.Security.PqxdhCorrectAndSecureδ, Security/HeadlineDelta.lean). See Computational/docs/TRUSTED_BASE.md.This file defines the mutual-authentication and first-message payload-privacy game (Auth), modeled after Fiedler–Günther (Eurocrypt 2025, Game 2) and Bhargavan–Jacomme–Kiefer–Schmidt (USENIX Security 2024), together with its key-schedule PRF and AEAD assumptions and closed-form advantage bound pqxdhAuthBound. In pqxdhAuthImpl1Key, .initPrin u samples a single Curve25519 identity scalar for both X25519 key agreement and XEdDSA signing (ikSignPk = ikAPk = P.x25519Base secIKA0, DEV-C5) and delegates all other queries to the shared two-key oracle body pqxdhAuthImpl true true (DEV-PQ13); Lemma 1 reads only the one-key advantage Auth1KeyAdvantage.\n\n\n\nKey-schedule and AEAD assumptions\n\nFirst-message AEAD (AeadIndCpa)\n\nstructure AeadIndCpaAdvL47–55 ↗#Two-stage adversary against one-time left-or-right IND-CPA of the first-message AEAD: choose outputs (ad, m0, m1, st) and dist guesses the challenge bit from st and the challenge ciphertext.\n\ndef AeadIndCpaAdv.BoundedL57–60 ↗#Query-budget compliance: choose and dist each make at most A.q_prim primitive queries.\n\ndef aeadIndCpaExpL62–71 ↗#One-time left-or-right IND-CPA experiment under uniform 80-byte MessageKeys; returns a uniform coin if m0.length ≠ m1.length.\n\ndef AeadIndCpaL73–75 ↗#One-time left-or-right IND-CPA advantage against the first-message AEAD.\n\nHMAC-SHA256 PRF (PrfHmac)\n\ninductive PrfHmacQueryL79–82 ↗#Evaluation query for PrfHmac.\n\ndef prfHmacSpecL84–85 ↗#Oracle interface for PrfHmac (32-byte outputs).\n\nstructure PrfHmacAdvL87–91 ↗#Distinguisher against the PRF security of hmacSha256Ref P k ·.\n\ndef PrfHmacAdv.BoundedL93–96 ↗#Query-budget compliance: at most A.q_prf PRF queries and A.q_prim primitive queries.\n\ndef prfHmacExpL98–117 ↗#Real-or-random PRF experiment for hmacSha256Ref P k · under a uniform 32-byte key k versus a lazy random function with 32-byte outputs.\n\ndef PrfHmacL119–122 ↗#PRF advantage against HMAC-SHA256 under a uniform 32-byte key, used for MessageKeys.macKey on the length-delimited aeadMacInput (DEV-PQ31) in first-message ciphertext integrity.\n\nChain-key PRF (PrfChainKey)\n\nstructure PrfCkAdvL126–130 ↗#Distinguisher for PrfChainKey on a real or uniform 32-byte pair (mkSeed, ckNext).\n\ndef PrfCkAdv.BoundedL132–134 ↗#Query-budget compliance: dist makes at most A.q_prim primitive queries.\n\ndef prfCkExpL136–149 ↗#Real-or-random PRF experiment for ck ↦ (chainKdfSeed ck, chainKdfNext ck) under a uniform 32-byte chain key ck.\n\ndef PrfChainKeyL151–153 ↗#PRF advantage against the chain KDF (chainKdfSeed, chainKdfNext).\n\nMessage-key PRF (PrfMsgKeys)\n\ndef effectiveHkdfSaltBytesL157–160 ↗#Effective 32-byte HKDF salt of an optional SPQR salt (none ↦ 0^32, some s ↦ s zero-padded to 32 bytes; RFC 2104 §2, RFC 5869 §2.2). For s.length ≤ 32, deriveMsgKeysRef at s? agrees with deriveMsgKeysRef at some (effectiveHkdfSaltBytes s?).toList.\n\ninductive PrfMsgKeysQueryL162–168 ↗#Evaluation query deriveMsgKeys(mkSeed, some salt) at a 32-byte effective salt (DEV-LS25). Indexing by {0,1}^256 avoids collisions between equivalent raw salts (none, some [], some 0^32) and matches the dual-PRF assumption on HMAC in its message slot (Bellare, Bernstein, Gajland, Saha, BBGS'23, Theorem 11).\n\ndef prfMsgKeysSpecL170 ↗#\n\nstructure PrfMsgKeysAdvL172–176 ↗#Distinguisher against PrfMsgKeys with access to derive and the primitive oracle.\n\ndef PrfMsgKeysAdv.BoundedL178–181 ↗#Query-budget compliance: at most A.q_prf PRF queries and A.q_prim primitive queries.\n\ndef prfMsgKeysExpL183–204 ↗#Real-or-random PRF experiment for deriveMsgKeys mkSeed (some ·) under a uniform 32-byte mkSeed versus a lazy random function into uniform 80-byte MessageKeys.\n\ndef PrfMsgKeysL206–208 ↗#PRF advantage against deriveMsgKeys(mkSeed, ·) over 32-byte effective salts.\n\nFirst root-KDF oracle\n\ndef evalRootKdfOrRoL212–225 ↗#Evaluates the first rootCreateChain(rk, dh) step of a session either concretely (useRo = false) or via a lazy caching random oracle (useRo = true).\n\nFirst-message authentication and privacy game\n\nstructure SentOPKEventL229–240 ↗#Recorded honest with-OPK send event for injective authentication matching.\n\nstructure SentNoOPKEventL242–252 ↗#Recorded honest no-OPK send event for injective authentication matching.\n\nstructure RecvdOPKEventL254–265 ↗#Recorded honest with-OPK receive event.\n\nstructure RecvdNoOPKEventL267–277 ↗#Recorded honest no-OPK receive event.\n\ndef matchSentOPKL279–292 ↗#Correspondence predicate between a RecvdOPKEvent and an unconsumed SentOPKEvent: compares EC keys up to cofactor (pow8), requires exact sb equality unless the receiver is corrupted (bCorr), and compares fixed-length pk1, ct, and m by byte equality.\n\ndef matchSentNoOPKL294–305 ↗#No-OPK counterpart of matchSentOPK (injective via (pqspkId, spkId, ekAPk) replay deduplication on receive).\n\nstructure AuthInitiatorEntryL307–321 ↗#Recorded initiator session metadata in AuthState.\n\ndef authInitCorruptionClauseL323–334 ↗#Post-game confidentiality freshness for a challenged initiator session: the peer was an uncorrupted honest principal at send time (s.fresh), and at game end either useRo = true and the peer identity key remains uncorrupted or one of (spk, opk, pqspk) was honestly generated, or useRo = false and pqspk was honestly generated.\n\ninductive AuthQueryL336–353 ↗#Adversary oracle queries in the Auth game: principal and pre-key management, left-or-right sends (sendOPK, sendNoOPK), responder receives (recvOPK, recvNoOPK), random-oracle queries (roQuery, roRootQuery), HNDL secret reveal (revealDhSecrets), and KOSK key registration (registerKey, DEV-PQ15).\n\ninductive AuthResponseL355–369 ↗#Oracle responses in the Auth game.\n\ndef authSpecL371–372 ↗#\n\nstructure AuthAdversaryL374–376 ↗#Adversary against the Auth game at setup S.\n\nstructure AuthStateL378–396 ↗#Mutable state of the Auth game.\n\ndef AuthState.emptyL398–415 ↗#\n\ndef runAuthSendL417–523 ↗#Executes an honest initiator first-message send (sendOPK or sendNoOPK). Encrypts m_secb when the peer is an uncorrupted honest principal (isFreshPeer) and m0 otherwise; leaks session keys only when the peer identity key is adversarial.\n\ndef consumeMatchingSentOPKL525–534 ↗#Marks the first unconsumed SentOPKEvent that matches r as consumed.\n\ndef consumeMatchingSentNoOPKL536–545 ↗#Marks the first unconsumed SentNoOPKEvent that matches r as consumed.\n\ndef pqxdhAuthImplL547–799 ↗#Two-key Auth oracle implementation (DEV-PQ13), parameterized by random-oracle mode useRo and authentication flag checkAuth.\n\ndef pqxdhAuthExpCoreL801–819 ↗#Core two-key Auth experiment (DEV-PQ13): wins outright if checkAuth ∧ finalSt.hasWonAuth, returns a uniform coin if any challenged session fails authInitCorruptionClause, and otherwise checks b' == secb.\n\ndef pqxdhAuthExpL821–824 ↗#Two-key Auth experiment (useRo = true, checkAuth = true).\n\ndef AuthAdvantageL826–828 ↗#Advantage |2 Pr[win] - 1| (boolBias) of A in pqxdhAuthExp.\n\nQuery-budget predicates\n\ndef isAuthRoQueryL832–834 ↗#\n\ninstanceL836–839 ↗#\n\ndef isAuthRoRootQueryL841–843 ↗#\n\ninstanceL845–848 ↗#\n\ndef isAuthSendQueryL850–853 ↗#\n\ninstanceL855–858 ↗#\n\ndef isAuthRecvQueryL860–863 ↗#\n\ninstanceL865–868 ↗#\n\ndef isAuthOnlineQueryL870–876 ↗#Online (non-random-oracle) game queries in AuthQuery S: every .inr q except the offline random-oracle queries .roQuery and .roRootQuery.\n\ninstanceL878–881 ↗#\n\ninstanceL883–895 ↗#Query-budget compliance for AuthAdversary S: bounds HkdfRO (b.q_ro), RootKdfRO (b.q_roRoot), initiator (S.N_send_tot), responder (S.N_recv_tot), total game (b.q_total), and online non-RO (b.q_on, isAuthOnlineQuery) queries. Every online query is a game query, so any adversary bounded at b.q_total satisfies q_on at { b with q_on := b.q_total }, and pqxdhAuthAdvantageBoundδ does not depend on q_on.\n\nClosed-form advantage bound\n\ndef rootKdfRoTermL899–903 ↗#Statistical boolBias term for the first-step root-KDF random oracle across S.N_sess_tot sessions and q_roRoot adversary .roRootQuery calls: bounds root-key guesses and session input collisions over 32-byte root keys.\n\ndef pqxdhAuthBoundL905–923 ↗#Closed-form boolBias advantage bound for Auth, which extends pqxdhKeyIndBound with:* 2 · N_send_tot · N_recv_tot / 2^250 for cofactor-8 X25519 collisions between fresh initiator ephemeral keys and prior responder receives;* rootKdfRoTerm S q_roRoot for the first root-KDF random-oracle step;* 2 · N_sess_tot · (epsPrfCk + epsPrfMsgKeys) for the chain-key and message-key PRF steps;* 2 · N_send_tot · epsAeadCpa for left-or-right payload privacy on fresh sends; and* 2 · (2 · N_sess_tot · epsPrfHmac + N_recv_tot / 2^64) for 8-byte HMAC tag forgery across at most N_recv_tot receives.\n\nOne-key Auth game\n\ndef pqxdhAuthExpCoreWithL927–945 ↗#Runs the Auth experiment of pqxdhAuthExpCore against an arbitrary authSpec S implementation impl.\n\ntheorem pqxdhAuthExpCore_eq_withL947–951 ↗#\n\ndef pqxdhAuthImpl1KeyL953–974 ↗#One-key Auth oracle (DEV-C5): .initPrin u samples a single Curve25519 scalar secIKA0 for both X25519 and XEdDSA (secIKAsign := secIKA0, ikSignPk := ikAPk = P.x25519Base secIKA0), and delegates all other queries to pqxdhAuthImpl true true S P.\n\ndef pqxdhAuth1KeyExpL976–979 ↗#One-key Auth experiment (pqxdhAuthExpCoreWith true true on pqxdhAuthImpl1Key).\n\ndef Auth1KeyAdvantageL981–986 ↗#Advantage |2 Pr[win] - 1| (boolBias) of A in pqxdhAuth1KeyExp.\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"Computational/Specifications/Pqxdh/Security/Auth/Game.lean","id":"/Tier-1___-statements/Statement-files-_LPAR_T_RPAR_/Computational.Specifications.Pqxdh.Security.Auth.Game/#file-Computational___Specifications___Pqxdh___Security___Auth___Game"},"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.CryptoGames.AEAD.EtMDefs/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___CryptoGames___AEAD___EtMDefs":{"contents":"Paper › LibsignalBinary/Specifications/CryptoGames/AEAD › EtMDefs.leanTier 2 · definitions\n\nTier 2 · definitions used by the computational proof and symbolic proof 5 headline theorems depend on it 51 of 341 lines trusted trust banner B3, trusted in part source ↗Read the definitions shown. Only the declarations that the headline statements use are shown, with the namespace, section, open and variable commands needed to read them. The collapsed runs are theorems, which Lean checks, and definitions that no headline statement uses.\n\nDeclarations on this page (8)structure CipherSchemestructure MACSchemeabbrev prfSpecstructure PRFStateabbrev PRFAdversarydef prfEvaldef prfGamedef prfAdvantage\n\nEncrypt-then-MAC AEAD from a one-time cipher and a PRF-based MACTrusted in part. The theorems of Computational/Theorems/ use only CipherScheme, MACScheme, PRFAdversary, PRFState, prfAdvantage, prfEval, prfGame, prfSpec from this file: read them. Everything else here is checked by Lean. See Computational/docs/TRUSTED_BASE.md.Defines one-time symmetric ciphers (CipherScheme, indcpaEncrypt, indcpaGame, indcpaAdvantage), deterministic MACs/PRFs (MACScheme), the Encrypt-then-MAC AEADScheme etm, the decryption-query bound MakesAtMostDecQueries, and the concrete reductions redCipher, redMACChall, and redMACForge used by etm_otcca (EtM.lean). Because the OT-CCA and IND-CPA games sample uniform ciphertexts from $ᵗ C, instantiations with AES-256-CBC fix the plaintext length (DEV-AEAD2).\n\n\n\nOne-time symmetric cipher (IND-CPA)\n\nstructure CipherSchemeL41–48 ↗#A deterministic one-time symmetric cipher. All randomness (e.g. key and IV in Signal's AES-256-CBC-PKCS#7) comes from the one-time key Key. Decryption returns Option Message to accommodate padding validation.\n\n10 declarations not shown (lines 54–122): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesCryptoGames.CipherScheme.Correct, CryptoGames.CipherScheme.indcpaSpec, CryptoGames.CipherScheme.INDCPAState.queried, CryptoGames.CipherScheme.INDCPAState.b, CryptoGames.CipherScheme.INDCPAState.key, CryptoGames.CipherScheme.INDCPAState, CryptoGames.CipherScheme.INDCPAAdversary, CryptoGames.CipherScheme.encryptQuery, CryptoGames.CipherScheme.indcpaEncrypt, CryptoGames.CipherScheme.branch, CryptoGames.CipherScheme.indcpaGame, CryptoGames.CipherScheme.indcpaAdvantage, CryptoGames.CipherScheme.indcpaAdvantage_eq_boolDist\n\nDeterministic MAC / PRF game\n\nstructure MACSchemeL128–132 ↗#A deterministic MAC / PRF with key space Key, input space Input, and tag space Tag (in Signal: 8-byte truncated HMAC-SHA256).\n\n\n\nabbrev prfSpecL138–140 ↗#PRF evaluation oracle Input →ₒ Tag.\n\nstructure PRFStateL142–146 ↗#PRF game state: key, hidden bit b, and the lazy table of the random function.\n\nabbrev PRFAdversaryL148–150 ↗#A PRF adversary, with its own coins (unifSpec) and the evaluation oracle Input →ₒ Tag.\n\n1 declaration not shown (lines 152–154): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesCryptoGames.MACScheme.evalQuery\n\n\n\ndef prfEvalL158–171 ↗#PRF evaluation oracle: b = false returns mac.tag key x; b = true returns a lazily sampled random function on Input → Tag.\n\n1 declaration not shown (lines 173–179): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesCryptoGames.MACScheme.branch\n\ndef prfGameL181–189 ↗#PRF hidden-bit game.\n\ndef prfAdvantageL191–194 ↗#Adv^{prf}_{mac}(A), the bias 2·|Pr[b = b'] − 1/2|.\n\n1 declaration not shown (lines 196–204): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesCryptoGames.MACScheme.prfAdvantage_eq_boolDist\n\n\n\n10 declarations not shown (lines 216–342): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesCryptoGames.AEADScheme.etm, CryptoGames.AEADScheme.isDecQuery, CryptoGames.AEADScheme.MakesAtMostDecQueries, CryptoGames.AEADScheme.cipherRedImpl, CryptoGames.AEADScheme.redCipher, CryptoGames.AEADScheme.challRedImpl, CryptoGames.AEADScheme.redMACChall, CryptoGames.AEADScheme.ForgeState.forged, CryptoGames.AEADScheme.ForgeState, CryptoGames.AEADScheme.ForgeState.chal, CryptoGames.AEADScheme.ForgeState.idx, CryptoGames.AEADScheme.forgeRedImpl, CryptoGames.AEADScheme.redMACForge\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"LibsignalBinary/Specifications/CryptoGames/AEAD/EtMDefs.lean","id":"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.CryptoGames.AEAD.EtMDefs/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___CryptoGames___AEAD___EtMDefs"}});